<Layout title="Linh Nguyen">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto pt-8 pb-16">
    <a href="/journals" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline"
      >&larr; Back to Journals</a
    >
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Language Myths
    </h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mb-8 italic">
      February 20, 2020
    </p>
    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-4">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Recently I've been reading a collection of sociolinguistics essays
        called <a
          href="https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/173095.Language_Myths"
          ><i>Language Myths</i></a
        >, which sets out to debunk popular misconceptions about natural
        language. More than once, it's completely upended preexisting beliefs I
        held about language. One salient example is the notion that certain
        languages are intrinsically more aesthetically pleasing than others -
        French is romantic, Italian is sophisticated, German is harsh, etc. You
        can point to specific phonemes to justify these claims (e.g., sharp
        consonant sounds in German, nasal vowels in Breton), but the authors
        argue that how a language sounds to you is vastly dependent on the
        context in which you hear it, as well as national and social identities.
        Cultural, historical, and personal associations and stereotypes remain
        the predominant factor.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        The idea that some languages or accents are inherently more attractive
        than others is known as the "inherent value hypothesis." There is little
        evidence supporting this and most language scholars are in favor of the
        opposing position known as the "social connotations hypothesis." This
        states that the perceived pleasantness of a language is a product of
        cultural context, historical preferences, and social conditioning, and
        is supported by far more evidence:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
        <li>
          Studies have shown that children enjoy listening to "non-standard"
          speech until they spend time in school.
        </li>
        <li>
          Neutral judges rate standard and non-standard varieties of languages
          equally favorably. (English speakers demonstrated no preference
          between Athenian vs. Cretan Greek or Parisian vs. Quebecois French,
          while Greek and French speakers had strong stated preferences for the
          Athenian and Parisian varieties.)
        </li>
        <li>
          When people were asked to rate how pleasant it would be to live in
          various cities/regions and the pleasantness of the accents of these
          cities/regions, the results were very highly correlated.
        </li>
        <li>
          Historically, the language variety of the ethnic elite or social elite
          of an era typically gets pushed via media, public policy, and language
          laws - all while suppressing non-prestige varieties. Once established,
          the language varieties of those in power become largely considered as
          "pleasant-sounding" or "beautiful" while they remain in power.
          (Old/Middle English was once viewed as ugly and barbaric. More
          accounts of praise for the sound of spoken English were recorded
          during the rise of the British colonialism.)
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Why does this matter? My personal takeaway is that internalizing this
        concept can help us understand how we treat people and make us kinder to
        one another. People who speak dialects associated with ethnic minorities
        are often ridiculed into believing that the way they speak (an integral
        part of who they are!) is inferior. Additionally, how someone speaks can
        have tons of social weight and it's crucial to check your biases when
        you're subtly influenced by a dialect or accent while in the position of
        choosing who to hire for a role or who to rent your apartment to.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        On a personal level, I'm reckoning with the fact that my
        "favorite-sounding" languages and dialects are more a reflection of how
        I view the people who speak them than any tangible, objective measure.
        For one, my distaste for the "harsh tones" of Vietnamese probably has
        more to do with internalized racism and stressful experiences with
        extended family than any properties of the language itself. I've heard
        many others describe Vietnamese as a beautiful language, particularly
        those with positive associations of Vietnam from travel or loved ones.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        I've also been thinking about the implications this has for how
        social/cultural context affects our interactions with art and people,
        including but not limited to:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
        <li>
          how we perceive the practices and doctrines of different religions
        </li>
        <li>the music we enjoy</li>
        <li>the people we find attractive</li>
        <li>the visual arts & architecture we find beautiful</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
